---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="About — KR4OGH pico balloons"
  description="How the KR4OGH pico balloon program works: WSPR telemetry, Traquito trackers, and a three-store data architecture."
>
  <div class="about prose">
    <h1>About the program</h1>
    <p>
      A pico balloon is a mylar party balloon carrying a solar-powered radio
      tracker weighing a few grams. Filled with just enough lift, it settles
      into a float in the stratosphere and rides the winds — sometimes for
      weeks, sometimes around the planet. There is no recovery and no control;
      once it leaves the ground, the only thing that comes back is radio.
    </p>
    <p>
      KR4OGH flies bone-stock
      <a href="https://traquito.github.io/">Traquito</a> trackers transmitting
      <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WSPR_(amateur_radio_software)">WSPR</a>
      on the 20&nbsp;m band at milliwatt power. Volunteer receiving stations
      around the world decode those transmissions and report them to
      <a href="https://wspr.live/">wspr.live</a>. Position, altitude, speed,
      solar-bus voltage, and processor die temperature are packed into pairs of
      WSPR messages using
      <a href="https://qrp-labs.com/u4b/u4bdecoding.html">U4B-style channelised telemetry</a>,
      which this project decodes with
      <a href="https://github.com/ryanmio/kr4ogh-pico">its own open-source stack</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Flight one</h2>
    <p>
      Two trackers, <strong>F1A</strong> (channel 206) and <strong>F1B</strong>
      (channel 348), launch the same morning as one experiment: same callsign,
      same dial frequency, different time slots. Launches are never announced
      in advance; flights appear here once they are in the air and reporting.
    </p>

    <h2>How a flight is scored</h2>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Pass</th>
          <td>Floats at 8,000&nbsp;m or higher, valid positions spanning at
            least 48 hours, and still transmitting after the first sunrise.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Fail</th>
          <td>No signal in the first 12 hours, or transmits on day one and
            never again after the first sunrise.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Wounded</th>
          <td>A reporting gap over 24 hours, presumed alive; the flight
            continues.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Closed</th>
          <td>Silence for more than 7 days.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Nightly silence is <em>not</em> a failure: the payload is solar-only and
      simply sleeps through the dark.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the data lives</h2>
    <p>This site is the visible end of a deliberate three-store split:</p>
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>SQLite — the record</th>
          <td>The authoritative flight record: raw spots (append-only), query
            coverage, decoded telemetry. Lives on the operator's machine,
            needs no network and no credentials.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Supabase — a cache</th>
          <td>A queryable hot copy of decoded telemetry that this site reads
            while a flight is in the air. Write-only from the tool, read-only
            for you, and allowed to pause or vanish with zero effect on the
            record.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>git — the archive</th>
          <td>When a flight closes, its full decoded track and an outcome
            write-up are committed to the repository. Archived pages here are
            built from those files and will render forever without any
            database.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Everything — decoder, channel table, ingest tool, this site, and the
      archived tracks themselves — is in the open at
      <a href="https://github.com/ryanmio/kr4ogh-pico">github.com/ryanmio/kr4ogh-pico</a>.
      The telemetry wire format and decode validation are written up in the
      repository's <code>docs/</code>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
